<!-- HOW ALUMINIUM AEROSOL CANS ARE MADE -->
<section class="section" id="how-aluminium-aerosol-cans-are-made">
  <h2>How aluminium aerosol cans are made</h2>
  <p>Aluminium aerosol cans are usually produced by impact extrusion from a circular slug. Impact extrusion
                forms a seamless body with good strength and no longitudinal seam; subsequent operations include
                trimming, washing, internal coating curing, necking (if required), printing/varnishing and end (dome)
                application. Each stage is controlled to tight dimensional and surface-quality tolerances because
                defects reduce burst strength and increase leak risk.</p>

            <p>Slug preparation: discs are blanked from coil or cast and cleaned prior to extrusion.</p>
            <p>Impact extrusion: a die forces the slug to flow and form a seamless can body and shoulder in one stroke.
            </p>
            <p>Trimming & washing: the formed cup is trimmed to length and washed to remove oils/particles before
                coatings.</p>
            <p>Internal lacquer application and cure: protective polymer coatings are applied and polymerized/cured at
                controlled temperatures to ensure adhesion and chemical resistance.</p>
            <p>Necking, doming and final dimensional checks: the can mouth is formed to accept standard valve sizes;
                dome and base geometry are verified for pressure performance.</p>
            <p>Quality control: wall thickness, ovality, burst/burst-pressure sampling, visual inspection and leak
                testing (water bath/pressure decay) are applied.</p>
