<!-- HOW TINPLATE AEROSOL CANS ARE MADE -->
<section class="section" id="how-tinplate-aerosol-cans-are-made">
  <h2>How tinplate aerosol cans are made</h2>
  <p>Tinplate aerosol cans originate from sheet steel that is tin-plated (electrolytic). Two major
                construction methods exist: two-piece (drawn-and-ironed or deep-drawn; seamless body + end) and
                three-piece (body formed by rolling and seam-welding, with separate top and bottom ends). After forming,
                cans are cleaned, internally coated, printed and seamed or fitted with ends; welded or mechanically
                seamed joints are inspected for hermeticity.</p>

            <p>Sheet cutting and body forming: sheets or coils are cut, blanked and formed by drawing or bending+welding
                depending on design.</p>
            <p>Welding / seaming (three-piece): longitudinal seams are welded or lap-seamed and finished; end seams are
                double-seamed.</p>
            <p>Internal coating: an internal lacquer is applied and cured to isolate the product from the steel
                substrate and tin layer.</p>
            <p>Printing, varnish and external finishing: inks and protective varnishes provide brand appearance and
                abrasion resistance.</p>
            <p>Inspection and leak tests: dimensional checks, seam integrity tests and pressure/leak testing are
                performed before shipment to fillers.</p>
