        <section id="regulatory">
</section>

<!-- REGULATORY AND SAFETY TEST REQUIREMENTS -->
<section class="section" id="regulatory-and-safety-test-requirements">
  <h2>Regulatory and safety test requirements</h2>
  <p>Aerosol containers are regulated as non-refillable pressure vessels in many jurisdictions; in the U.S.
                common specifications are DOT 2P and 2Q which prescribe construction and performance tests. Industry
                guidance (FEA, national regulators) requires routine burst/pressure testing, leak testing and safe
                handling of flammable ingredients. Fillers and can manufacturers perform bench and destructive testing
                to confirm containers meet rated working pressure and burst thresholds.</p>

            <p>DOT specifications: 2P/2Q classifications define materials and test pressure ranges for disposable
                aerosol containers.</p>
            <p>Typical tests: normal-temperature pressure test, high-temperature pressure test, burst test, seam
                integrity and leak detection (water bath/pressure decay).</p>
            <p>Industry guidance: FEA’s safety guidelines and national transport regulations govern flammable propellant
                handling, facility design and emergency planning.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="problems">
</section>

<!-- COMMON MANUFACTURING PROBLEMS AND HOW THEY ARE SOLVED -->
<section class="section" id="common-manufacturing-problems-and-how-they-are-solved">
  <h2>Common manufacturing problems and how they are solved</h2>
  <p>Manufacturing and filling lines can experience defects that affect safety and shelf life — examples
                include coating adhesion failure, can paneling (buckling), crimp leakage, and seam defects. Each issue
                has specific root causes (material quality, forming speeds, coating cure, crimp tooling mismatch) and
                standard corrective actions such as process parameter adjustments, tooling maintenance, and enhanced
                inspection plans.</p>

            <p>Coating adhesion failures → check substrate cleanliness, bake profiles and coating chemistry.</p>
            <p>Paneling / buckling → ensure correct wall thickness, dome geometry and filling pressures are within rated
                limits.</p>
            <p>Crimp leakage → verify valve flange dimensions, crimp tooling condition and crimp height/roll pressure.
            </p>
            <p>Seam defects (tinplate) → non-destructive seam inspection and destructive seam testing to root-cause
                welding/rolling issues.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="choosing">
</section>

<!-- CHOOSING ALUMINIUM OR TINPLATE FOR YOUR PRODUCT -->
<section class="section" id="choosing-aluminium-or-tinplate-for-your-product">
  <h2>Choosing aluminium or tinplate for your product</h2>
  <p>Selecting the right can depends on product chemistry, pressure requirements, cost and branding. Aluminium
                offers lighter weight and seamless bodies (good for higher finish and certain product compatibilities);
                tinplate provides established supply chains and cost advantages for some formats but requires robust
                internal coatings for corrosive products. Choose based on compatibility testing (chemical resistance),
                mechanical performance and total landed cost including coating and filling adjustments.</p>

            <p>Aluminium is often preferred where weight reduction, corrosion resistance with correct coatings, or
                premium appearance is desired.</p>
            <p>Tinplate is commonly used where two- or three-piece formats, different seam/forming options or specific
                price points make sense.</p>
            <p>Always run compatibility tests (coating × product), pressure lifecycle tests and accelerated aging for
                your specific formulation.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="conclusion">
</section>

<!-- CONCLUSION -->
<section class="section" id="conclusion">
  <h2>Conclusion</h2>
  <p>Aluminium and tinplate aerosol cans are produced by distinct, well-established industrial processes
                (impact extrusion vs sheet forming/seaming) and require specific finishing, coating and testing regimes
                to ensure safety and product compatibility. For filling-line operators and product developers, close
                coordination between can choice, valve specification and filling process parameters is essential to
                safe, reliable, and cost-effective aerosol production.</p>

            <p>Final checklist: verify substrate and coating compatibility, test crimp and pressure performance, and
                follow applicable regulatory testing before commercial runs.</p>
        </section>

